On 2008-05-19, Zeitgeist <tomfhaines@gmail.com> wrote:
> On May 18, 4:56 am, Derek Ray <de...@moot.its.only.a.spamtrap.org>
> Would a potential solution be that polypiling weapons/armour reduces
> their effectiveness? e.g. Polymorphing +5 boots of molasses, would result in
> a random piece of +4 armour. This would also pretty much eliminate the
> tactic of over enchanting several missile weapons and then polymorphing them.
>
> I'm not sure if this idea is something that you'd be interested in
> implementing, but I thought I'd suggest it anyway.

I have actually considered this in the past, though with the
more-aggressive approach of having the positive enchantment on
weapons/armor come out the other end as rnd(X), where X was the original
'plus'; so a +7 shortsword could come out as anywhere between +1 and +7.
(This leaves a certain amount of temptation available to polypilers,
while at the same time introducing an element of risk into the equation.)

I'm still not sure the arguments presented are convincing me of anything
other than "polypiling may be a problem", though.  The "gifts" from god
are being presented in this thread as though they are always armor, but 
there is a 50% chance that any given gift will be a weapon -- and while 
those may make the world easier for a player in the short term, they don't 
fall into the "permanent utility" category (since most people still
prefer to ascend with their chosen artifact).

